+### RESULT-0018 — Replayed human play trades early points for a late harvest; the bot does the reverse
+
+- **type:** result
+- **status:** accepted as an exploratory record; **not** accepted as a tested finding
+- **scope:** the 10 recordings in `recordings/`, one player (the owner), 4 candidate configs, seeds 1/2/10/777; describes recorded play only and changes no level, target, rule, or bot parameter
+- **statement:** The recordings are the only primary record of human play in this project and had never been analysed. Nine of the ten replay **exactly** against `solver/engine.js` — final score, move count, and every individual move's points — which is the gate for everything below; the tenth is dropped because its candidate config (`5b12806fa862`) is no longer in the repo. At each of the 135 replayed positions every legal chain was enumerated with `enumerateLegalChains` to establish the exact best available move. **Over a whole game the human and the bot capture nearly the same share of it — 0.515 against 0.587 — but the curves cross.** By thirds of the move budget the human takes 0.426 / 0.447 / **0.685** of the best available while the bot takes 0.629 / 0.590 / **0.537**: the human starts worse and finishes far better, and the bot degrades. Per-move points move the same way, the human roughly doubling across a game while the bot stays flat. The mechanism is visible in lattice discipline — the share of moves whose sum lands on a power of two (`FACT-0006`). Early the two are alike (human 73%, bot 79%); by the final third the human has **dropped to 35% while the bot holds at 84%**. That discipline buys future options, and in the endgame there are none left to buy, so the human cashes out into the largest chain available and the bot keeps paying for an option it will never exercise. On 41 of 135 moves the human took under a quarter of what was available; those chains average 6.5 tiles at sum 692 and 33 of 41 land on a power of two. **These setup moves come in runs of up to six**, so the harvest is the first ordinary move that ENDS a run, compared against ordinary moves no run preceded: **13.2 tiles at sum 3,779 for 18,208 points, against 14.8 tiles at sum 1,584 for 7,623**. Both sides sit above the nine-tile multiplier cap, so the multiplier is identical at 5 and the sum and score ratios are equal at **x2.39** — the entire gain is chain sum, not chain length. Tested separately: the sum difference clears the project bar at **t = 3.45**, while the length difference is **-1.6 tiles at t = -1.25** and is not distinguishable from zero. Length is therefore reported as unchanged, and neither "longer" nor "shorter" is supported. **No dose-response is established.** Harvest sum by build length is 3,419 (n=12), 3,936 (n=6), 4,352 (n=2), 2,048 (n=1) and 7,744 (n=1); only the first two carry any weight, the n=1 five-move case moves against the trend, and the 7,744 that makes the sequence look cumulative is a single observation. Excluding the one game the owner played under the since-fixed input bug (`RESULT-0009`) sharpens every figure rather than softening it: 0.567 against 0.583 overall, 0.479 / 0.758 human against 0.621 / 0.541 bot early-to-late, and **all 30** declines landing on a power of two.
+- **evidence:** `solver/human-replay.js`; `.orch/human-replay-01.json` (the full 135-move table: human, bot, and exact-best points, chain length, sum, and lattice status per position); `recordings/`; `solver/exact-score.js` `enumerateLegalChains` (the enumerator behind `RESULT-0003`).
+- **proof_class:** `direct_source` for the replays themselves and for the exact best-available figures, which are enumerated rather than sampled; `hypothesis` for every claim about what the pattern means. One player on four boards bounds nothing about human play in general.
+- **pre_registration:** **NONE. This analysis was exploratory throughout and no part of it tests a hypothesis.** Unlike `RESULT-0019`, nothing here was declared before the data was seen. Every analytic choice was made afterwards: the threshold defining a setup move (under 25% of the best available) was picked while looking at the table; the run-grouping was corrected only after the owner challenged a conclusion drawn from the uncorrected one; the two alternative definitions (chains under nine and under seven tiles) were tried after the first was disputed; and the significance tests were run after the conclusion they support had already been asserted. **The dataset is now spent for confirmatory purposes** — all ten recordings have been examined, so no holdout remains within it. The line between what is measured and what is chosen runs at the per-move table: the 135 rows in `.orch/human-replay-01.json` carry no analyst degrees of freedom, while every grouping built on top of them does. Read the table as evidence and the story as a hypothesis awaiting a pre-registered test on recordings that do not yet exist.
+- **as_of:** 2026-08-28
+- **reverify:** `node solver/human-replay.js --from .orch/human-replay-01.json` re-derives every grouping from the saved table in about a second. To rebuild the table itself, `node --max-old-space-size=8192 solver/human-replay.js --out <path>`; expect 135 moves across 9 games with `44d3802d` dropped, the early/late capture crossover, and the late lattice gap near 35% against 84%. That takes roughly half an hour — a full 5x7 opening holds over 8 million distinct actions.
