+- **notes:** This narrows but does not close the standing note that the reference bot is a weak proxy for a skilled player (`RESULT-0008`). It gives the first move-by-move comparison on a common scale, and the direction is the one already recorded: where they differ, the human is ahead. It does not size the margin — one player, four boards, and that player designed the game. The owner states the intent directly: take a small chain to set up a bigger one. **The data supports that description as given.** An earlier reading in this project recorded the harvest chain as *shorter* than a typical move; that was an artifact of grouping, because 19 of the 41 moves following a setup move are themselves setup moves, and averaging them into the harvest dragged its length down. Grouped correctly the harvest chain is the same length as an ordinary one and worth 2.39x as much. The lever is chain sum: `chainMultiplier` is capped at 5 from nine tiles, both groups are past that cap, and 77 of the 111 clean moves were already at or past it, so length had nothing left to earn. The magnitude is sensitive to how a setup move is defined, and this must not be over-read: the x2.39 figure uses a detector that conditions on the enumerated best move, which is large precisely when a big chain is nearby, so it partly selects for the harvest it measures. Under a purely mechanical definition that never references the optimum — any chain under nine tiles, which forgoes the maximum multiplier by construction — the same three-group structure holds at **x1.55**, and under seven tiles at x1.29. **The pattern is not an artifact of the detector; the headline ratio is a property of it.** Treat roughly 1.5x as the defensible figure. The heavy tiles are built by earlier moves folding several tiles into one survivor, which is a cross-move setup and not a preference expressible inside one walk — see `RESULT-0019` for the attempt to express it inside the walk and why it fails. **What a confirmatory test would require, declared here so it cannot be chosen to fit a later result:** the setup-move definition fixed in advance at chains under nine tiles, because it is mechanical and never references the enumerated optimum; the comparison fixed as harvest-versus-ordinary under the run-grouping above; the predicted effect a harvest chain sum near **1.55x** ordinary at a length difference indistinguishable from zero; and fresh recordings that no one has looked at, on boards not among these four. None of that exists yet.
