+### RESULT-0019 — Taking the heaviest tile past the multiplier cap is worse at every threshold
+
+- **type:** result
+- **status:** accepted
+- **scope:** `solver/bot.js` `CHAIN_HEAVY_AFTER` / `heavyAfter`, added and left at **0** (disabled); the shipped bot is unchanged and no target, level, or receipt moves
+- **statement:** `RESULT-0018` shows the owner's harvest chains carry a far larger sum than ordinary moves at essentially unchanged length. The hypothesis tested here was that the walk should therefore stop preferring the lightest reachable tile once the length multiplier is maxed: `chainMultiplier` caps at 5 and reaches it at nine tiles, so past that an added tile contributes exactly its own value x 5 and the walk is still deliberately choosing the smallest tile it can reach. **The hypothesis is false, and not marginally.** Flipping the walk to heaviest-first at thresholds 7, 9, 11 and 13 measures **-32.65% (t = -10.7), -16.06% (t = -8.1), -10.10% (t = -6.5) and -6.57% (t = -4.3)** against the shipped bot, paired per (level, seed) with the standard error clustered by level, on 12 levels x 40 unseen seeds. Win rate falls from 99.17% to 70.83% at threshold 7. Every arm is significant in the wrong direction against the project's own `t > 3` bar, and the damage is **monotone in the threshold** — flipping later hurts less — so the gradient points at never flipping, which is the bot that already ships. The cause is the chain rule (`FACT-0001`): a chain extends on equal-or-double, so values never decrease along it, and taking the heaviest reachable tile raises the floor every later tile must clear. Tiles that large are scarce, so the walk dies on the spot. The real trade is not value against nothing but **value against the chain surviving**, and survival keeps paying past the cap because each further tile is still worth its own value x 5 — length continues to pay, linearly rather than multiplicatively. The reasoning that "the multiplier is capped" implies "length stops paying" is the specific error.
+- **evidence:** `solver/heavy-after-ablation.js`; `.orch/heavy-after-ablation-01.txt` (the screen table); `solver/bot.js` `CHAIN_HEAVY_AFTER = 0`; `solver/engine.js` `buildGreedyChain` and `buildGreedyPathBeam` `heavyAfter` option; `solver/calibration.js` pins `heavyAfter: 0`.
+- **proof_class:** `heuristic_observation` — a measured degradation of this heuristic player at four thresholds. It does not exclude some other way of raising the chain sum; it excludes doing it inside the walk by tile preference.
+- **as_of:** 2026-08-28
+- **reverify:** `node solver/heavy-after-ablation.js`; expect every threshold negative with |t| > 3 and the lift rising monotonically toward zero as the threshold rises. `node --test solver/tests/*.test.js`; expect 236 tests, 233 pass, and only the three known receipt failures.
